], \"unknowns\": [\"whether the local record overwrite can be combined with the transfer vote bypass and an abstaining voter to produce two leaders in one term, which needs the other voters' records to stay empty for that term\"]},\n\n162:    \"rationale\": \"Both core relationships and their connection are explained from actual code: formation and advancement are grounded in the vote tally, the pre-vote round, commitment.match/recalculate and the startIndex own-term rule; context and authority are grounded in the term/state update, leader identity update, the leader lease step-down and TimeoutNow. The connection is sourced: votes are only re-granted for the same term, older-term RPCs are ignored, match indexes are only usable at or after startIndex, and a transfer requires the target to be caught up. Three premises that earlier versions of this map left as unknowns are now recorded as mechanisms in the behaviors that own them: the transport heartbeat callback performs its term, state and leader writes without being serialized with the main goroutine and publishes the term through a plain atomic store; a client future for VerifyLeader or a batched Apply carries no shutdown escape once a completed Shutdown has left the buffered request channels unprocessed; and the candidate's self-vote is written for the proposed term without comparing it with the vote record, so a published term that has dropped by one makes the next election propose a term that already holds a vote and overwrite it. The counter-parts that bound each mechanism are recorded beside them (the fast path cannot append logs or advance the commit index; the default unbuffered applyCh always completes and the leader lease and the replication path cover term discovery; the vote-granting path still refuses a second different vote in one term), so no core gap is claimed and the overview stays usable.\"\nRATIONALE LINE 162 len 1648\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
